// Lore for points on the warhammer-fantasy maps. See src/data/lore/index.js.
export const WARHAMMER_FANTASY_LORE = {
  // Reikland
  'fw-altdorf': 'Seat of the Emperor and the Colleges of Magic, five centuries on from the Old World map’s telling and grown fatter on trade for it. The Empire it commands is larger and louder than it once was, and no less quarrelsome for the extra centuries of Elector politics.',
  // Wissenland
  'fw-nuln': 'Still the Empire’s forge and gunnery school generations later, its foundries turning out the artillery that has kept pace with every new horror the world has produced since. An army without Nuln steel behind it fights this era at a real disadvantage.',
  // The Wasteland
  'fw-marienburg': 'By this era a fully independent city-state in all but occasional Imperial pretence, richer than ever and answerable to its own Directorate rather than to any Elector. Its merchant fleets have interests as far as Lustria, and its loyalty is for sale to whoever is paying.',
  // Middenland
  'fw-middenheim': 'The Fauschlag still stands unconquered by siege, its cult of Ulric as fierce in this age as in the last. Five hundred years of history have only added more sieges to the list the city likes to tell around its winter fires.',
  // The Empire
  'fw-nordland': 'A colder, more crowded coast than it once was, its fishing towns grown wary after generations of deeper Norscan raids. The province still has no great city of its own, only a shoreline someone always has to watch.',
  'fw-ostland': 'Forest and frontier, still bleeding soldiers to the beastherds and worse things that come down out of the north in numbers the province has never managed to fully stop. Its counts remain some of the grimmest in the Empire, with good reason.',
  'fw-hochland': 'Hill country and hunting ground, its riflemen’s reputation only sharper after centuries of practice on the game and the greenskins both. It remains thin on people and heavy on the woodcraft that has kept it standing.',
  'fw-talabheim': 'The crater city endures, its single guarded tunnel still the only sane way in after five more centuries of sieges that failed to prove otherwise. It has outlasted enough enemies by now that its people treat the impregnability as simple fact rather than boast.',
  'fw-ostermark': 'A harder frontier than ever, worn down generation after generation by the greenskins spilling out of the Worlds Edge Mountains. Its levies are seasoned by necessity, not by choice.',
  // Stirland
  'fw-wurtbad': 'Stirland’s capital where the Old Dwarf Road crosses the River Stir, still the only legal market for the province’s western wine by ancient Imperial decree. Centuries deeper into the era, it remains wealthier than Stirland’s grim reputation suggests.',
  // The Empire
  'fw-averland': 'Cavalry country on the Empire’s southern plain, its horse-lords still riding to whichever war the Emperor calls loudest. Centuries of border skirmishing with the Border Princes have only sharpened its riders further.',
  'fw-solland': 'A province in name only by this era, its lands ravaged so badly by orc invasion generations back that no Elector Count has sat its throne since. What remains is contested ground between Averland’s ambitions and the ruin the greenskins left behind.',
  // Sylvania
  'fw-drakenhof': 'Sylvania’s cursed seat five centuries deeper into its history of undead lords and living despair. Whoever claims Drakenhof Castle inherits a county that has never fully belonged to the living.',
  // Middenland
  'fw-drakwald': 'The Great Forest’s dark heart, no less unmapped and no less full of beastmen than it was five hundred years earlier. Armies still lose men to it who are never found.',
  // The Forest of Loren
  'fw-athel-loren': 'The forest still decides who enters and who leaves, its Wood Elves no more inclined to explain themselves to outsiders than they were generations ago. It remains territory in name only; the wood holds itself.',
  // Bretonnia
  'fw-couronne': 'Still the site of the Lady’s first appearance to Gilles le Breton, and still the dukedom every claimant to Bretonnia’s throne wants behind them. Its knights ride to errantry from the same shrine their ancestors did.',
  'fw-bastonne': 'One of Bretonnia’s oldest and proudest dukedoms, its knights’ reputation only more storied for the centuries added since the kingdom’s founding. No claim to the throne has ever succeeded without its support.',
  'fw-carcassonne': 'A fortress dukedom whose walls have outlasted sieges that would have levelled most others, its reputation for endurance now centuries deep. Any royal faction wants it secured before making a move.',
  'fw-lyonesse': 'A founding dukedom of the realm, its fields as fought-over in this era’s succession disputes as they ever were. Its knights carry old weight at court simply for the name.',
  'fw-quenelles': 'A forested dukedom bordering Athel Loren, its uneasy truce with the elves within still holding by tradition more than trust. The peace here has always been one incident from unravelling.',
  'fw-bordeleaux': 'A wealthy coastal dukedom, its vineyards and fleet making its dukes as much merchant princes as knights even generations on. Its port remains as valuable as its chivalry.',
  // Kislev
  'fw-kislev-city': 'The Tzarina’s walled capital, still standing sentinel over the last great city before the true north after further centuries of holding that line. Every Chaos incursion since has had to reckon with its gates.',
  'fw-erengrad': 'Kislev’s great port, wealthier and more cosmopolitan than the realm around it, its trade with the Empire only grown in the centuries since. It remains easier to tax than to arm.',
  'fw-praag': 'Twice broken by Chaos hosts in the earlier era, and no stranger to further horror in the centuries since. Its ruined quarters and the things that are said to still move in them make it as much warning as city.',
  // Norsca
  'fw-norsca-heart': 'The heartland of the Norscan tribes, its fjords and longhouses producing raiders the Old World has learned to dread on a schedule. Chieftains here measure standing in ships launched and favour shown by the Dark Gods.',
  'fw-norsca-west': 'The Bjornling coast of Norsca, its clans more inclined toward raiding the Empire’s northern shore than toward the deeper Chaos worship of tribes further north. Even so, no Old World coast within reach of their longships sleeps easily.',
  'fw-norsca-east': 'The frontier reaches of Norsca, closer to the Chaos Wastes and correspondingly stranger for it. Tribes here are shaped by the same tides of dark magic that warp the land itself.',
  // Northern Wastes
  'fw-chaos-wastes-north': 'The true north, where the land no longer pretends to obey the world’s ordinary rules. Tribes that live here have made mutation a way of life, because nothing else survives this close to the Gate.',
  // Border Princes
  'fw-border-princes': 'A patchwork of self-declared princedoms south of the Empire, no less lawless five centuries on than it ever was. Every ambitious sellsword with a captured keep still calls himself a prince here.',
  // Tilea
  'fw-tilea': 'A land of feuding city-states bound by shifting treaties and older vendettas, its condottieri companies as ready to switch sides this century as any before it. No single power has ever managed to unite it for long.',
  'fw-miragliano': 'Famous across Tilea for its canals and its inventors, home to engineers whose devices are as likely to start the next war as end it. Its condottieri have seized the city outright before, and are assumed to be considering it again.',
  'fw-sartosa': 'A pirate-run island city, nominally a Tilean principality but governed in practice by whoever commands the most cutthroats in port that season. Its harbour serves smugglers as readily as any legitimate trade.',
  // Estalia
  'fw-estalia': 'A fractured kingdom of rival lords more often at odds with one another than united behind their king. Its knights and privateers are for hire to whoever meets the price.',
  'fw-magritta': 'The royal capital of Estalia, the one city with a plausible claim to speak for a kingdom otherwise split among quarrelling nobles. Holding it is the closest thing to holding Estalia at all.',
  // Badlands
  'fw-badlands': 'Ancestral greenskin country south of the Worlds Edge Mountains, no calmer after centuries than it was before. Waaaghs! still gather here the way storms gather, and break wherever the horde’s momentum happens to carry it.',
  'fw-black-crag': 'The great Orc fortress built over the bones of the Dwarf hold of Karak Drazh, still unretaken generations after it first fell. Its garrison treats every attempt against it as sport, same as always.',
  // World's Edge Mountains
  'fw-karaz-a-karak': 'The Everpeak, the high king’s seat, still held after millennia by Dwarfs who have never once considered abandoning it. The Book of Grudges kept within is thicker now than it was five centuries earlier, and no less binding.',
  'fw-karak-kadrin': 'The Slayer cult’s greatest shrine, still guarding Peak Pass and still drawing every Dwarf who has sworn the Slayer’s oath and wants a worthy death. Centuries have not shortened the queue.',
  'fw-eight-peaks': 'Once Vala-Azril-Ungol, still fought over by Dwarfs, Night Goblins and Skaven who have never let five hundred more years of bloodshed settle the question of who owns it. The ruined halls are worth exactly as much now as they were when the fighting started.',
  'fw-zhufbar': 'The forge-heart of the Karaz Ankor, its furnaces still turning Karak Varn’s gromril into the finest metalwork the Dwarf race produces. Every hold downriver still depends on what comes out of it.',
  // World's Edge Mountains
  'fw-black-fire-pass': 'The one wide road between the Empire and the Badlands, still remembered for the horde Sigmar broke here at the Empire’s founding. Every Waaagh! that has tried to prove that victory was luck has failed the same way.',
  // The Blighted Marshes
  'fw-skavenblight': 'The rotting Skaven capital beneath the Blighted Marshes, the Council of Thirteen still scheming under the Horned Rat’s shadow five centuries on. Humans still insist it does not exist, which still suits the Skaven perfectly.',
  // The Great Ocean
  'fw-albion': 'A mist-shrouded isle far off the Old World’s northwest coast, said in old Truthsayer legend to have once tutored the ancestors of elves, dwarfs and men alike. Almost no expedition that lands on its bogs and standing stones returns to confirm or deny it.',
  'fw-great-ocean-isle': 'A scatter of lesser islands in the Great Ocean’s western reaches, useful chiefly as waypoints for ships bold enough to attempt the crossing to Lustria or Naggaroth. Whoever controls them controls a resting point no long voyage can do without.',
  // Ulthuan
  'fw-lothern': 'The great port-capital of Ulthuan and seat of the Phoenix King, its harbour said to be among the largest in the world and its fleets the backbone of High Elf power. Whoever threatens Lothern threatens the throne itself.',
  'fw-eataine': 'The kingdom surrounding Lothern, its wealth and its navy underwriting much of Ulthuan’s influence beyond its own shores. It is as close to a heartland as the ten kingdoms have.',
  'fw-caledor': 'The proudest and most martial of the High Elf kingdoms, its dragon-riding nobility descended from the line of Caledor Dragontamer himself. Its warriors are sent wherever Ulthuan’s wars are worst.',
  'fw-saphery': 'The kingdom of mages, home to the Tower of Hoeth where High Elf magic is taught and its lore kept. Whoever threatens Saphery threatens the accumulated knowledge of the entire race.',
  'fw-tor-yvresse': 'A kingdom of scholars and shipwrights among Ulthuan’s inner realms, its counsel valued in the courts of the Phoenix King even when its armies are not the largest. Old Yvressian wisdom carries weight beyond its size.',
  'fw-chrace': 'A wild kingdom of woodland and hill, home to the White Lions who guard the Phoenix King and to hunters as comfortable stalking prey as fighting wars. Its warriors are prized wherever Ulthuan needs its fiercest defenders.',
  'fw-avelorn': 'Home to the Everqueen and her Sisters of Avelorn, a realm of ancient forest treated as close to sacred ground by every other kingdom on the isle. Its safety is bound up with the fertility rites that keep all of Ulthuan blooming.',
  'fw-ellyrion': 'Home of Ulthuan’s horse-lords, its riders considered the finest cavalry the High Elves field. Its people learn the saddle before they learn much else.',
  'fw-cothique': 'An outer kingdom of vast, brooding pine forest, more exposed to raids from beyond Ulthuan’s shores than the sheltered inner realms. Its people have a harder, more watchful temperament for it.',
  'fw-tiranoc': 'A kingdom largely sunken beneath the ocean since Nagarythe’s fall, its remaining coast a reminder of how much Ulthuan lost during the Sundering. What is left is jealously held all the same.',
  'fw-nagarythe': 'The ruined Shadowlands, once the proudest of the inner kingdoms before Malekith’s betrayal and the Sundering left it desolate. Little grows here now except old grief and older grudges.',
  'fw-armheim': 'A remote holding within Nagarythe’s shadowed borders, exposed to whatever still moves through the ruined kingdom around it. It is watched more than it is governed.',
  'fw-gaen-vale': 'A secluded vale within Avelorn’s forest, sheltered by the same old magic that protects the Everqueen’s realm at large. Few outsiders are ever permitted to see it.',
  'fw-blighted-isle': 'A cursed scrap of land off Ulthuan’s coast, touched by whatever dark sorcery scarred it long ago and shunned by sailors since. Nothing about it invites a landing, which is exactly why something occasionally tries.',
  // Naggaroth
  'fw-naggarond': 'The Tower of Cold, Naggaroth’s capital and Malekith’s own seat of power. It is the coldest, cruellest court in the world, and the only one that matters if the Witch King is to be answered at all.',
  'fw-clar-karond': 'The Tower of Doom, principal dockyard of the Dark Elf fleets, where slaves haul the timber that becomes Naggaroth’s next raiding fleet. Its shipwrights keep Malekith’s wars supplied with hulls.',
  'fw-hag-graef': 'The Black Crag, a sinister city at the bottom of a sunless canyon, ruled by whichever noble house has most recently murdered its way to the top. Its intrigues are as dangerous as any battlefield in Naggaroth.',
  'fw-karond-kar': 'The Tower of Despair, also called Slaver’s Gate, battered constantly by the Sea of Chill’s storms. Slaves taken across the ocean pass through here before being broken to Naggaroth’s use.',
  'fw-ghrond': 'The North Tower, Naggaroth’s bastion against the Chaos Wastes, garrisoned heavily enough to turn back incursions that would overwhelm a lesser fortress. It is as much shield as it is city.',
  'fw-har-ganeth': 'The City of Executioners, given wholly to the worship of Khaine, the Bloody-Handed God. Few places in the world treat ritual slaughter with as much reverence.',
  // Lustria
  'fw-itza': 'The oldest Lizardmen city, first founded by the Old Ones themselves and still treated as the holiest ground in Lustria. Its Temple-Cities elsewhere all trace their purpose back to what was first built here.',
  'fw-hexoatl': 'The City of the Sun, seat of Lord Mazdamundi, the oldest living Slann, on the isthmus that anchors Lustria’s jungle empire. Its geomantic web underlies the magic that holds the whole continent’s order in place.',
  'fw-tlaxtlan': 'The City of the Moon, one of Lustria’s great Lizardmen temple-cities, its Saurus legions defending it with the same patient, ancient discipline as any Slann-ruled city. Explorers who reach it rarely leave with what they came for.',
  'fw-chaqua': 'A Lizardmen city deep in the Lustrian jungle, its ruins as dangerous to plunder as any temple on the continent. The gold that occasionally reaches the Old World from here explains why anyone keeps trying.',
  'fw-vampire-coast': 'The domain of Luthor Harkon and the undead pirate fleets he commands, cursed since a stolen coffin washed ashore here centuries ago. Its harbours serve the dead the same way ordinary ports serve the living.',
  'fw-skeggi': 'The oldest human settlement in Lustria, founded by Norse raiders chasing the same gold that draws every other Old World expedition here. It has survived the jungle longer than most colonies manage.',
  // Nehekhara
  'fw-khemri': 'The ancient capital of Nehekhara, seat of the Tomb Kings and once the greatest city of the earliest human civilisation. Its treasuries and its risen legions both make it a prize no sane army takes lightly.',
  'fw-zandri': 'A great city of Nehekhara west of the Worlds Edge Mountains, its tombs as thick with grave goods as its rival cities’. Tomb robbers who succeed here rarely enjoy the profit for long.',
  'fw-lahmia': 'The first city of vampires, cursed since its queen first drank the black blood of the ancients, and reduced to ruin and legend since Nehekhara’s armies burned it. What is left is a warning as much as a ruin.',
  'fw-lybaras': 'A city of scholars and healers in eastern Nehekhara, once restored to greatness under Alcadizzar and Khalida’s rule. Its libraries held knowledge the rest of the desert kingdoms coveted even in life.',
  'fw-land-of-the-dead': 'The desert that swallowed Nehekhara’s fertile heartland after Nagash’s curse fell upon it, now home only to rival tomb-city legions and the odd desperate expedition. It has been a graveyard for longer than it was ever a garden.',
  'fw-great-mortis-delta': 'The delta where Nehekhara’s great river reaches the sea, once the breadbasket of the ancient kingdoms and now as blighted as the desert around it. Its ruins are picked over by whoever is bold enough to risk the crossing.',
  'fw-shifting-sands': 'A treacherous stretch of Nehekharan desert whose dunes are said to move enough to swallow whole caravans. Guides who claim to know the safe paths through it are trusted at the traveller’s own risk.',
  // The Southlands
  'fw-devils-backbone': 'A jagged mountain spine along the Southlands’ coast, home to little but the hardiest scavengers and the odd shipwreck’s survivors. Its name is not thought to be an exaggeration by anyone who has sailed past it.',
  'fw-serpent-coast': 'A humid, disease-ridden stretch of the Southlands’ shore, its jungles hiding both Lizardmen ruins and the sort of predators that keep most expeditions from ever going inland. Traders who risk it come back with goods no one else can supply.',
  'fw-oyxl': 'A Lizardmen holding on Lustria’s fringe, smaller than the great temple-cities but no less fiercely defended. Its Saurus garrisons treat every trespasser the same way the greater cities do.',
  // Araby
  'fw-al-haikk': 'The City of Thieves, whose Sultan is acknowledged as overlord of Araby’s other city-states when the kingdom manages to agree on anything. Its markets and its intrigue are equally famous.',
  'fw-lashiek': 'An Arabyan port city, its wealth built on the same trade in grain, spice and slaves that sustains the rest of the sultanates. Its Sultan’s loyalty is negotiable, like most things in Araby.',
  'fw-martek': 'An Arabyan city-state whose Sultan’s ambitions have occasionally reached beyond its own walls at its neighbours’ expense. Rival sultanates watch it as warily as they watch any foreign fleet.',
  'fw-copher': 'A lesser Arabyan port, useful chiefly for the caravan roads it feeds into and the customs it collects from ships too small to make for the greater cities. It survives on tolls rather than conquest.',
  // The Dark Lands
  'fw-zharr-naggrund': 'The Tower of Zharr-Naggrund, capital of the Chaos Dwarf empire, a black ziggurat said to have been sorcerously carved from a single mountain. Its forge-hammers never stop, and neither does the labour of the slaves that feed them.',
  'fw-plain-of-zharrduk': 'The volcanic plain surrounding Zharr-Naggrund, worked ceaselessly by Chaos Dwarf industry and the Hobgoblin labour that serves it. The smoke over it never fully clears.',
  'fw-mount-grimfang': 'A peak within the Dark Lands claimed by Chaos Dwarf holdings, its slopes as inhospitable as the empire that mines them. Nothing about it welcomes an outsider.',
  // Ogre Kingdoms
  'fw-mountains-of-mourn': 'The brutal highlands of the Ogre Kingdoms, still named for a grief no one living can fully explain. Its tribes fight for meat and glory in equal measure, exactly as they always have.',
  // Grand Cathay
  'fw-great-bastion': 'The colossal wall Grand Cathay raised along its northern border to hold back Hobgoblins, Hung and the forces of Chaos alike. Its garrisons and animated defences have held for centuries, and losing any stretch of it opens the empire behind to everything the wall was built to stop.',
  'fw-celestial-lake': 'A vast lake at the heart of Grand Cathay, central to the empire’s farmland and its internal trade routes. Whoever commands its shores commands a great deal of Cathay’s food.',
  'fw-wei-jin': 'A province of Grand Cathay, its farmland and garrisons part of the machinery that keeps the empire fed and defended. It answers to the Dragon Emperor’s court like every other province, in form if not always in enthusiasm.',
  'fw-nan-gau': 'A southern province of Grand Cathay, its distance from the Great Bastion giving it a somewhat gentler history than the provinces further north. It still owes the same tribute and the same loyalty.',
  'fw-shang-yang': 'A Cathayan province built around trade and craft, its workshops supplying goods that move along the empire’s internal roads and out through its ports. Its wealth makes it worth more than its garrison alone reflects.',
  'fw-qiang': 'A frontier province of Grand Cathay, closer to the wild country beyond the empire’s settled heart and correspondingly more militarised. Its people are used to short notice and long watches.',
  'fw-hanyu-port': 'One of Grand Cathay’s gateway ports, where the empire’s tightly controlled trade with the outside world is permitted to pass. Foreign merchants who reach it still answer to Cathayan customs on Cathayan terms.',
  'fw-xing-po': 'A coastal holding of Grand Cathay, its fisheries and modest trade feeding into the empire’s wider economy. It is unglamorous, and no less necessary for it.',
  'fw-jinshen': 'The Wastelands of Jinshen, a scarred stretch of Cathayan territory where old wars or older sorcery left the ground unfit for much beside garrison duty. It is held mostly to deny it to whoever else might want it.',
  'fw-broken-lands-tian-li': 'The Broken Lands of Tian Li, a fractured stretch of Grand Cathay’s frontier, difficult to hold and easy to raid. Its garrisons rotate often and rarely by choice.',
  'fw-mount-li': 'A mountain within Grand Cathay’s borders, its slopes worked for what minerals they hold and watched for what might come over them. It is unremarkable except to the people who have to hold it.',
  // Ind
  'fw-ind': 'A vast, wealthy subcontinent south of Cathay, ruled by its own princes and famed in the Old World for spice, silk and the sheer scale of its armies when it chooses to field them. Few outsiders have ever seen enough of it to say what its real limits are.',
  // Nippon
  'fw-nippon': 'An island empire east of Cathay, its warrior culture and closed ports making it as mysterious to Old World traders as Cathay itself, if not more so. Little of its internal politics reaches the west intact.',
  // Southern Wastes
  'fw-chaos-wastes-south': 'The southern mirror of the Chaos Wastes, no less warped by the tides of dark magic than the northern reaches. Whatever tribes survive here have adapted to it the same brutal way their northern cousins have.',
  // Norsca
  'fw-vanaheim-mountains': 'A mountain range within Norsca’s territory, its passes as treacherous as the tribes that use them. It offers little beyond the strategic value of controlling who moves through it.',
  // Kislev
  'fw-troll-country': 'A blighted stretch of Kislev’s northern frontier, thin on farmland and thick with the trolls that give it its name. Armies still cross it only because the roads north offer nothing better.',
  // Northern Wastes
  'fw-mountains-of-naglfar': 'A range within the Northern Wastes twisted by proximity to the Chaos Gate, its stone said to shift the way the tribes’ loyalties do. Nothing about it maps consistently for long.',
  // The Dark Lands
  'fw-gash-kadrak': 'A scarred stretch of the Dark Lands, its ground fought over by Chaos Dwarfs, Hobgoblins and whatever else the region breeds. It offers little beyond ground worth denying an enemy.',
  'fw-chimera-plateau': 'A high plateau within the Dark Lands, named for the monstrous things said to nest there. Few expeditions that go looking for the source of the name come back to confirm it.',
  'fw-the-skull-road': 'A grim overland route through the Dark Lands, marked by enough bones to explain its name many times over. Caravans that use it accept the risk because the alternative routes are worse.',
  'fw-ancient-giant-lands': 'A remote stretch of the Dark Lands said to be the ancestral home of the giants that occasionally wander into more settled country. What lives there now mostly avoids outsiders, and outsiders return the favour.',
  'fw-bone-road': 'Another of the Dark Lands’ grim trade routes, its name earned the same way the Skull Road’s was. It remains in use because trade with the Chaos Dwarfs has to travel somehow.',
  // World's Edge Mountains
  'fw-rib-peaks': 'A jagged stretch of the Worlds Edge Mountains, its passes fought over by Dwarfs and greenskins in the usual way of this range. Nothing about the fighting here has changed much in centuries.',
  'fw-black-water': 'A flooded stretch of the Worlds Edge Mountains near Karak Varn, named for the dark water that drowned the hold’s tunnels generations ago. Skaven are said to still work the flooded galleries beneath it.',
  'fw-peak-pass': 'The great eastern trade road through the Worlds Edge Mountains, guarded by Karak Kadrin and used by anyone willing to brave the mountains rather than go around them. It has carried trade and armies both for as long as anyone has kept count.',
  'fw-deadrock-gap': 'A narrow mountain pass named for the barren stone around it, useful chiefly to whoever wants to move through the Worlds Edge Mountains without being seen from the main roads. It is exactly as inhospitable as it sounds.',
  'fw-the-wolf-lands': 'A wild stretch of the Worlds Edge Mountains’ foothills, home to more wolves and worse things than people. It is claimed more often than it is actually controlled.',
  // Border Princes
  'fw-death-pass': 'A notoriously dangerous pass through the Border Princes’ hill country, its name earned the ordinary way in a region full of contenders for it. Every self-styled prince has a story about what it cost them to use it.',
  'fw-blood-river-valley': 'A valley in the Border Princes soaked in the blood of countless small wars between rival warlords. It is decent ground by local standards, which is reason enough to keep fighting over it.',
  // Badlands
  'fw-eastern-badlands': 'The eastern half of the Badlands, no less thick with Orc tribes than the western reaches, closer to the Border Princes and correspondingly more often raided from both directions. Waaaghs! gather here as readily as anywhere else in the region.',
  'fw-western-badlands': 'The western Badlands, bordering the Dark Lands and the Chaos Dwarf holdings within them, adding another layer of danger to the usual greenskin chaos. Few travellers cross it without an escort.',
  'fw-marshes-of-madness': 'The fetid Marshes of Madness, still said to drive men and greenskins to violence faster than the surrounding Badlands alone would explain. Armies that camp here rarely leave in better order than they arrived.',
  // The Southlands
  'fw-blightwater': 'A poisoned stretch of the Southlands’ coast, its water unfit to drink and its shoreline correspondingly empty of the settlements that dot healthier coasts nearby. Whatever ruined it has never been fully explained.',
  'fw-the-barrier-idols': 'A line of ancient standing stones along the Southlands’ coast, carved by hands no living scholar can identify. Sailors give them a wide berth on old superstition alone.',
  // Bretonnia
  'fw-parravon': 'A mountain dukedom in the Grey Mountains’ foothills, its knights as much rangers as chivalry given the rough country they patrol. Its passes still connect Bretonnia to the Empire, and still matter for exactly that reason.',
  'fw-mousillon': 'The City of the Damned, ruined and plague-struck since its last duke’s death centuries earlier and no more reclaimed now than it was then. Whatever still lives in it has kept every other duke from formally taking the title.',
  // Naggaroth
  'fw-ironfrost-glacier': 'A vast glacier beyond Naggaroth’s watchtowers, its winds said to blow in from the Realms of Chaos themselves and its cold sharp enough to kill outright. Nomadic Hung tribes hunt mammoth across it and each other besides, and holding its approaches is the only warning Naggaroth’s heartland gets before the Chaos Wastes arrive in force.',
  'fw-road-of-skulls': 'The overland route to Har Ganeth, the City of Executioners, where Crone Hellebron enforces murder as a state monopoly the rest of the year. Whoever secures the road controls the flow of sacrifice and pilgrimage alike to Khaine’s bloodiest shrine.',
  'fw-deadwood': 'A bleak seaside province at Naggaroth’s far northeastern edge, its frozen capital guarding the entrance to the Sea of Chill. Whoever holds it controls the sea lane, not the comfort of the land around it.',
  'fw-spiteful-peaks': 'A mountain range north of Naggarond that has held back the Chaos Wastes for as long as anyone can measure. Whatever comes through its passes reaches the Witch King’s capital next, which makes the range worth more than its bare rock suggests.',
  'fw-iron-mountains': 'The range that shelters Naggarond in its eastern foothills, dividing the Boiling Sea from the Sea of Malice. Dark Pegasi nest in its heights, and any force that wants Naggarond intact has to take these slopes first.',
  'fw-iron-foot-hills': 'The spite-choked hinterland from which Naggarond’s black spires rise, closer to the Witch King’s seat than any Dark Elf noble finds comfortable. Whoever camps here camps within sight of Malekith’s own walls.',
  'fw-iron-coast': 'A desolate shore west of the Iron Mountains, its volcanic vents and ill reputation keeping away even Dark Elves who are not easily discouraged. The Altar of Ultimate Darkness is said to lie somewhere in its hills, which is exactly the kind of rumour that draws the reckless.',
  'fw-black-flood': 'A sparsely held estuary of the Sea of Chill between Naggarond and Clar Karond, with Hag Graef its only real settlement. It is the road two of Naggaroth’s great cities must use to reach each other, and closing it closes a great deal else besides.',
  'fw-granite-hills': 'Sheltered hinterland around Karond Kar and Hag Graef, its good land left to watchtowers and monuments because working it honestly counts as weakness among Dark Elves. Blacklight Tower and Morathi’s petrified rivals stand watch over ground that could feed a city, if anyone here were willing to be seen trying.',
  'fw-broken-lands': 'An exposed stretch of Naggaroth’s eastern coast, open to attack from the sea but just as useful for launching one. Nagarythe’s exiles took root here first, and anyone who wants a foothold on this coast still has to go through them.',
  'fw-obsidian-peaks': 'The industrial heart of Dark Elf shipbuilding, where black timber hauled from the Doom Glades is turned into the hulls that raid every coast in the world. Clar Karond depends on what these ridges produce, and so does every fleet Naggaroth sends to sea.',
  'fw-red-desert': 'An isolated stretch of barren sand known for giant spiders, escaped Greenskin slaves, and the odd lost Nehekharan who wandered further than intended. Nobody claims it for its soil, only for the strays and the salvage that wash up in it.',
  'fw-clawed-coast': 'A remote stretch of eastern Naggaroth where the armourer Hotek built his citadel after Ulthuan cast the Dark Elves out. His Forgebound kin still guard whatever devices he left behind, and the secrets inside are worth more than the coastline they sit on.',
  'fw-witchwood': 'A magical forest touched by a living World Root, one of the few patches of green in all Naggaroth that has not been logged for warships. The Heralds of Ariel guard it as something closer to sacred than strategic, which has never stopped others from wanting it anyway.',
  'fw-doom-glades': 'The greener, softer reaches of southern Naggaroth, worked by lesser Dark Elf clans through slave plantations rather than conquest. It is unglamorous ground, but its timber feeds the shipyards that keep the rest of the realm armed.',
  'fw-sunken-lands': 'A drowned fringe of coastline that implies a larger Naggaroth once stood here before the sea, or the Sundering, took the rest. What is left is marsh and memory, held mainly to deny it to whoever might use it as a landing.',
  'fw-ironsand-desert': 'A high, barren plateau linking the Doom Glades to the Ashen Coast, one of the few approaches into southern Naggaroth that does not require a fight first. That alone makes it worth garrisoning, whatever else it lacks.',
  'fw-titan-peaks': 'An interior upland where an ancient catastrophe turned a swathe of forest to stone, leaving petrified trees standing over ground no one has fully explained. Whatever caused it once could presumably do it again, which keeps most settlers at a respectful distance and most treasure-hunters closer than they should be.',
  'fw-bleak-coast': 'Slave plantations and logging camps along southern Naggaroth’s milder shore, its largest town a former High Elf colony Malekith seized in person. Holding ground the Witch King took himself carries its own kind of weight.',
  'fw-ashen-coast': 'A long, lifeless stretch of grey shore on southern Naggaroth’s western edge, ending near the Boiling Sea. The Black Way opens from here into the Sea of Malice, and Dark Elf raiders have used that door to reach Lustria before; nobody has managed to nail it shut.',
  'fw-forbidding-coast': 'The reef-torn shoreline fronting the Doom Glades, its waters as unforgiving as its name promises. Ships that miss the safe channel here do not get a second attempt, which makes local knowledge of the coast worth more than any chart.',
  // Northern Wastes
  'fw-frigid-wasteland': 'A stretch of the Northern Chaos Wastes as bleak as its name promises, where taiga gives way to tundra and tundra gives way to nothing recognisable as land at all. Nothing grows here that was not warped into growing, and nothing that survives the winters owes its shape to natural law.',
  'fw-northern-wastes': 'The near edge of the true Chaos Wastes, where taiga and tundra still resemble the world before the corruption sets in deeper north. It is the last stretch of ground that can be crossed without expecting the landscape itself to turn hostile, which is precisely why armies use it as a staging ground.',
  'fw-shard-lands': 'A broken country at the western edge of the Northern Wastes, where Naggaroth’s last watchtowers give out and the world itself starts to crack and reform. Something about the scent of cruelty that clings to the place draws the attention of Slaanesh in particular, and little that comes here leaves unchanged.',
  'fw-shard-coast': 'The shattered shoreline beneath the Shard Lands, where the ground’s habit of splintering and reforming does not stop at the waterline. Ships that anchor here trust the coastline at their own risk, and few captains trust it twice.',
  'fw-noisome-tumour': 'A diseased stretch of the Northern Chaos Wastes, its Bilious Cliffs and Forest of Decay serving Nurgle’s purposes the way healthier ground serves everyone else’s. The Tower of Flies stands watch over swamps that spread a little further every season, and stopping that spread is reason enough for anyone still capable of disgust.',
  'fw-plain-of-illusions': 'A stretch of the Northern Wastes where nothing seen can be entirely trusted, its Crystal Spires and Port of Secrets built on ground that answers to Tzeentch’s taste for deception. The Lucent Maze nearby is said to hold answers scrawled by something that already knows every question worth asking.',
  'fw-bloodfire-falls': 'A miles-long flow of magma pouring from a volcanic plateau deep in the Northern Chaos Wastes, sacred to Khorne’s worshippers for the heat and fury it never stops producing. The Howling Citadel above it is said to open onto the Forge of Souls itself, which is precisely the kind of claim that draws champions who cannot resist testing it.',
  'fw-blood-marshes': 'A Chaos-corrupted wetland on the edge of the Frozen Sea, its waterways choked with blood that never quite dilutes. The fortress of Snaergrvold sits at its heart, and the Norscan tribe that holds it has learned to treat the smell of death as simply the smell of home.',
  // Norsca
  'fw-cold-mires': 'A vast tract of fen and unholy swamp across the Sea of Chaos north of Norsca proper, the only known home of the Toad Dragons that lurk in its black water. Bile Trolls infest it in numbers found nowhere else, descended from the stock that followed Tamurkhan south when he set out to claim the Throne of Chaos.',
  'fw-helspire-mountains': 'Southwestern Norsca’s volcanic range, named for the active peak at its centre and rich in the obsidian that draws tribes despite the ground’s habit of erupting under them. The Graeling and the Varg have never settled which of them owns it, and neither shows any sign of stopping.',
  'fw-gianthome-mountains': 'Norsca’s eastern border, a pitiless range of eternal winter where little survives except Giants, Trolls, and the Dwarf Slayers who come looking for a worthy death among them. The Dwarfs who split from their own kind to raise Kraka Drak here grew strange enough, by dwarfish standards, that the rest of the Karaz Ankor still eyes them with suspicion.',
  'fw-trollheim-mountains': 'A south-eastern range claimed by the shipbuilding Sarl tribe, its Tower of Khrakk serving as a staging post for raids into Kislev. Riders return here loaded with plunder every year, which is reason enough for the Sarl to keep crossing ground as dangerous as Troll Country to do it.',
  // Kislev
  'fw-black-blood-pass': 'A mountain pass on Kislev’s frontier, named for the amount of it that has been spilled fighting over who gets to use the road. Every army moving between Kislev and its neighbours eventually has to decide whether the pass is worth the toll in blood its name promises.',
  'fw-goromadny-mountains': 'Kislev’s eastern bulwark, a hard frontier crossed centuries ago by the Gospodar steppe peoples who took the realm from the Ungols already living there. Norscan Dwarfs held this ground once, and enough of their old works remain that adventurers still come looking for what was left behind.',
  'fw-eastern-oblast': 'Steppe and pine forest running east from Praag to the mountains, its capital Volksgrad growing rich on the Cathayan trade passing through. It stands between two threats at once, Chaos and Norscan warbands pressing from the north and Greenskin raiders from the east, which makes holding it a full-time occupation.',
  'fw-cursed-city': 'Praag, set apart from the ordinary business of Kislev as a province unto itself after twice being broken by Chaos hosts. What still moves in its ruined quarters makes the city as much a warning as a prize, and taking it means taking whatever came with it.',
  'fw-southern-oblast': 'Kislev’s mildest ground and its main farming country, sitting south of the Lesser Tobol where the land finally softens into something worth ploughing. Southern Kislevites are called soft for it by their northern kin, and answer the insult with some of the finest lancers the Tzarina can field.',
  // The Dark Lands
  'fw-kdatha': 'A plateau said to lie somewhere on the edge of the Northern Wastes, north of the Dark Lands proper, its exact location shifting like a mirage no matter how carefully it is sought. Champions of every Chaos god have marched toward it for an age, since legend holds that whoever proves supreme among its bone-strewn ruins takes a step toward uniting all four under one banner.',
  'fw-blasted-wastes': 'A great desert in the western Dark Lands, its ecosystems long since scoured away by winds that never let up. Nomadic Goblin tribes survive here by ambushing caravans and each other in roughly equal measure, which is as much order as the region has ever managed.',
  'fw-desolation-of-azgorh': 'The wreckage left when the volcano Azgorh blew itself apart centuries ago, its earthquakes and pyroclastic flows carving the ground into razor rock, sulphur pits and vents of poison steam. Nothing about it invites settlement, which is exactly why the Chaos Dwarfs found it convenient to claim.',
  'fw-howling-wastes': 'A flat, desolate plain in the eastern Dark Lands, swept by a thin wind that carries something like voices whenever it rises. The Chaos Dwarfs test their newest ordnance here precisely because almost nothing lives close enough to complain, and the Sentinels mark the one useful thing about the place: the crossing of three trade roads.',
  'fw-plain-of-bones': 'The furthest southern reach of the Dark Lands, a desert of coloured sand where the rib-cages and skulls of dragons rear up many times the size of a man. It is where dragons of an older, larger age are said to have come to die, and whatever draws scavengers to graveyards draws them here as well.',
  'fw-mouth-of-ruin': 'Where the River Ruin meets the Sea of Dread southwest of the Mountains of Mourn, its water choked yellow with Chaos Dwarf effluent from the mines upstream. The trading post built on stilts above the mire still turns a profit ferrying goods along the Spice Route, which says something about what merchants will tolerate for the right price.',
  'fw-path-to-the-east': 'An unnamed, winding valley in the northern Mountains of Mourn, growing colder and stranger the further it climbs toward the Chaos Wastes. Ice drakes and the hardiest Ogre tribes call the border their own, and anyone moving between the Ogre Kingdoms and the true north has no choice but to go through them.',
  'fw-zorn-uzkul': 'A frigid, wind-scoured plateau in the northern Dark Lands, its dusty ground littered with the bones of the ancient dead. Uzkulak, built here before the Great Catastrophe even split the world, hides a bustling slave port behind a secretive inner city that has never fully explained itself to outsiders.',
  // The Empire
  'fw-middle-mountains': 'A modest range at the Empire’s heart, marking the border between Nordland, Ostland, Hochland and Middenland, and notorious for holding no mineral wealth worth the mining. Chaos gained a foothold here during the Great War against it and was never fully driven out, leaving fortresses like Brass Keep to watch pockets of corruption the Empire has simply learned to live alongside.',
  'fw-mootland': 'A small, fertile Electoral province wedged between Stirland, Averland and Sylvania, granted its own vote after Emperor Ludwig the Fat was fed one meal too good to ignore. Its Halfling farmers keep it densely settled and comfortably fed, which makes it soft, rich, and exactly the kind of neighbour bigger provinces resent.',
  // Middenland
  'fw-misty-hills': 'Some of the most remote country in the Empire outside the well-guarded Erengrad-Marienburg Road, left to Greenskins and outlaws for want of anyone else willing to live there. Somewhere beyond its furthest hills lies the Black Pit, a sunless valley of piled bones and spider-lairs that even the region’s usual squatters know to avoid.',
  // Nordland
  'fw-laurelorn-forest': 'A reclusive forest on the Empire’s northern coast, claimed on paper by both Nordland and Middenland and defended in practice by neither, since the Eonir who actually live there answer to nobody. Descended from High Elves who never left the Old World, they have held this ground since before either province existed to argue about it.',
  // Reikland
  'fw-northern-grey-mountains': 'The Empire’s share of the range dividing it from Bretonnia, where the well-patrolled passes give way to a wide, murky country scarcely anyone bothers to travel. What lives in its unwatched stretches has never had much reason to introduce itself, and the mineral wealth seamed through its eastern slopes has never had much reason to go unmined.',
  // Wissenland
  'fw-southern-grey-mountains': 'Dwarf country older than the Empire itself, its Grey Dwarf holds like Karak Azgaraz plainer and poorer than the great karaks of the Worlds Edge Mountains but no less stubbornly held. The eastern kin who coined the name meant it as an insult, and the holds have worn it as a badge of endurance ever since.',
  'fw-wissenland': 'The Empire’s southwestern county and the home province of Nuln, whose foundries make it the armoury the rest of the realm depends on. Barren and flinty toward its mountainous west, fertile toward its east, it is a province that pays for its poor half with the wealth of its rich one.',
  // Border Princes
  'fw-forest-of-gloom': 'A thousand-mile belt of forest near the Black Mountains, home to the largest arachnids found anywhere in the Old World and the spider-riding Goblins who have learned to live alongside them. Beastmen prowl its darker reaches too, and whatever fails to avoid or kill the giant spiders first does not usually get a second try.',
  'fw-eastern-border-princes': 'The eastern half of the Border Princes, rich and fertile and correspondingly fought over by every warlord who can raise a company. Only Barak Varr and the trading town of Akendorf stand between this stretch and the Badlands across the Black Gulf, which makes it the Empire’s last soft southern door.',
  'fw-the-vaults': 'A mountain range where the Black Mountains meet the Grey, forming the one natural border shared by Tilea, the Border Princes, Bretonnia and the Empire all at once. Less monster-infested than the ranges around it, it has become a refuge for Dwarf clans who lost their eastern holds and had nowhere else left to dig.',
  // World's Edge Mountains
  'fw-silver-road': 'The western end of the Ivory Road, where the ancient Old Dwarf Road finally earns its later name as it climbs toward the four-hundred-foot gates of Karaz-a-Karak. Every caravan that reaches the Everpeak by land has to survive this stretch first, which makes it worth more in tolls than most provinces are worth outright.',
  'fw-southern-worlds-edge': 'A dry, contested range where the Badlands, the lower Dark Lands and the wider Worlds Edge Mountains all meet, currently held by the Dwarfs of Karak Azul from their plateau-top hold. Its master runesmith has marched south chasing the lost hold of Karak Zorn, leaving his castings to keep the mountain safe in his absence, which every rival watching the border is well aware of.',
  'fw-northern-worlds-edge': 'The northern stretch of the range, linking Kislev and Ostermark to Peak Pass and the Blasted Wastes beyond, with the treacherous High Pass as its only way through in the far north. Everything moving between the Old World and the lands past Kislev by this route has to survive these mountains first.',
  // Bretonnia
  'fw-forest-of-arden': 'A vast oak forest climbing the Grey Mountains’ lower slopes, seat of the Dukes of Artois and the site of the first Gathering of the Companions that legitimised Bretonnia’s fourteen dukedoms. Orcs and worse still use its depths as a staging ground for raids, which has never stopped it from being ground every claimant to the old unity wants behind him.',
  'fw-forest-of-chalons': 'A Bretonnian woodland bleeding so gradually into Athel Loren that even the locals cannot say exactly where one forest ends and the other begins. Its Sacred Lake is where Gilles and his Companions are said to have first met the Lady, which makes the forest’s ownership a matter of legitimacy as much as land.',
  // The Forest of Loren
  'fw-torgovann': 'The Forge of Starlight, a High Realm of Athel Loren ruled by Lord Daith and home to the artisans who make the forest’s tools of wonder and war. Vaul’s Anvil stands here, and whoever wants Athel Loren armed for anything beyond arrows has to reckon with what this realm alone can produce.',
  'fw-wydrioth': 'The Pine Crags, a High Realm shaped from skycrown oaks and rockbriar into citadels that pass for fortresses even in a forest that prefers not to build. Constantly beset by Dwarfs and Greenskins spilling down from the Grey Mountains, it has learned to treat siege as a permanent condition rather than an event.',
  'fw-talsyn': 'The King’s Glade, seat of Athel Loren’s ruling line and the beating political heart of the Wood Elves. Whoever threatens Talsyn threatens the forest’s leadership directly, which is a different order of provocation than raiding its edges.',
  // Estalia
  'fw-irrana-mountains': 'A largely barren range shared uneasily between Bretonnia and Estalia, its passes guarded by fortress towns like Lovarre against the brigands and Goblins who otherwise have the mountains to themselves. The trade road between Bilbali and Magritta runs straight through it, and nobody controls that road without controlling these peaks first.',
  'fw-estalia-coastline': 'The long, harbour-studded coast that has made Estalia a nation of sailors and privateers as much as knights. Hundreds of merchant hulls sail from it daily under a dozen quarrelling lords, and taxing that traffic is worth more to most Estalian nobles than any single stretch of land behind it.',
  // The Blighted Marshes
  'fw-blighted-marshes': 'A reeking morass stretching from the northern Tilean sea to the foot of the Black Mountains, its black pools and mazed channels hidden under a permanent, choking mist. Somewhere in its rotten heart the Skaven Under-Empire has its foundations, and death here comes quickly enough to the unwary that most outsiders never learn exactly where.',
  // The Great Ocean
  'fw-galleons-graveyard': 'An island built around a sentient whirlpool at the centre of the Great Ocean, pulling entire galleons into its maw the way lesser currents pull driftwood. Count Noctilus keeps his court here amid the wreckage, and the drowned dead of every ship it has claimed are said to rise again in its service.',
  // Badlands
  'fw-southern-badlands': 'A mix of stinking bog and parched scrub fought over by Savage Orc tribes, its Galbaraz marshes one of the few places in the world where true Oathgold can still be mined. High Elves once held a colony here and lost it during the War of the Beard, which tells its own story about how well outsiders tend to keep a grip on this ground.',
  'fw-broken-teeth': 'A mountainous, forgotten stretch east of the Southern Worlds Edge Mountains, its twisted heart hiding Cripple Peak, better known to the wider world as Nagashizzar. Whatever draws the ambitious to a half-mile-high citadel built on necromantic ambition has never been in short supply, and the mountains around it have swallowed most who came looking.',
  // Ulthuan
  'fw-southern-yvresse': 'The harder half of a kingdom already hardened by Grom the Paunch’s invasion generations past, its folk proud of soil that other Elves consider bleak. The mist-wreathed Shifting Isles sit close enough offshore that the locals have learned not to trust every shape those waters conjure, real or otherwise.',
  // Araby
  'fw-pirate-coast': 'The scummiest waters in the world by common consent, where Arabyan freebooters work the rocky coves alongside Sartosan corsairs and Border Princes cutthroats. No single power has ever claimed it outright, which suits everyone currently profiting from the fact that nobody has.',
  'fw-coast-of-araby': 'Araby’s principal seat of trade and contact with the wider world, home to the Sultan’s capital of Al-Haikk and the rival wealth of Copher’s markets. Every foreign merchant who wants a foothold in Araby has to make peace with this coast first, because there is no other door in worth using.',
  'fw-atalan-mountains': 'Araby’s most prominent range, dividing the coastal cities from the interior deserts and feeding the torrents that keep those coasts fertile. Its mineral wealth has drawn enough skirmishing that mines like Eye of the Panther are remembered as much for their battles as their diamonds.',
  'fw-land-of-assassins': 'A shadowy, more insular Araby than the cosmopolitan coast to the north, its city of Lashiek built on marble and the slave trade in equal measure. Sorcery is practised openly here, and the Wizard Caliph’s reputation for laying and lifting curses is exactly the sort of local talent that keeps rivals from testing the province directly.',
  'fw-great-desert-of-araby': 'A burning plain the Arabyans call the Sahra, sparsely populated by bedouin and Ogre tribes who survive on hard-won knowledge of its few oases. The Harmattan wind can bury the unwary under dunes for days at a stretch, but the desert still commands the routes to the Gulf of Medes, which keeps it worth crossing.',
  'fw-land-of-the-dervishes': 'Founded by Old World knights who stayed behind after the Great Crusade against Araby rather than sail home, its city of Sudenberg still sees crusaders and treasure hunters passing through on their way south. The Gulf of Medes gives it a defensible, well-watered position that has kept the settlement alive long after the crusade that built it ended.',
  // The Southlands
  'fw-western-jungles': 'Sweltering tropical forest largely abandoned by the Lizardmen until the ruined city of Tlaqua was recently reclaimed as a base for the Old Ones’ will. Goblin tribes moved into the vacuum in the meantime, bringing spider worship with them, and neither side shows much interest in sharing what was left behind.',
  'fw-heart-of-the-jungle': 'A magical forest deep in the Southlands, home to the reclusive Bowmen of Oreon and touched by the same living current that runs through Athel Loren and the Witchwood. Few outsiders find it, and fewer still are welcomed once they do.',
  'fw-southlands-worlds-edge': 'The southern tail of the great mountain range, said to hide Karak Zorn, the very first Dwarf hold ever raised, somewhere in its unmapped reaches. Giant venomous spiders infest the slopes around Mount Arachnos and have drawn goblin cultists who worship them, which has done nothing to discourage Thorek Ironbrow’s expedition to reclaim what his ancestors abandoned.',
  'fw-central-jungles': 'A vast, untracked sprawl of jungle that has swallowed most explorers who ventured south from Araby, Sudenberg or the Land of the Dead looking for plunder. The Lizardmen who still hold it have drifted from the wisdom of Lustria’s greater Slann, and what guides them now answers to older instincts than prophecy.',
  'fw-golden-pass': 'A winding break through the Great Mountains that split the Southlands north from south, dominated near its summit by a colossal tower built entirely of gold. The Old Ones raised it for reasons long since lost, but the gold itself has never needed a reason to be worth taking.',
  'fw-crater-of-the-waking-dead': 'A desert crater where the Tomb Kings Imanotep and Ushtep still refight a rivalry mortal death was supposed to end, cursed by the gods to wage it forever. Every skeleton cut down here mends itself and rises again within the crater’s saturated magic, which makes it a battlefield that never actually runs out of soldiers.',
  'fw-kingdom-of-beasts': 'A jungle territory southeast of Rasetra that has changed hands between Lizardmen and Tomb Kings more times than either side cares to count. Whatever gave it that name has never mattered as much as the fact that it keeps getting fought over regardless.',
  'fw-shifting-mangrove-coastline': 'Miles of mangrove swamp resting on a sargasso of roots and decay along the Southlands’ eastern coast, less inhospitable than the interior jungles but dangerous enough that even Elves avoid it when they can. Sartosan sailors have charted pieces of it, and pieces are all anyone has ever managed.',
  'fw-southern-jungles': 'An isolated, peaceful stretch of the Southlands holding Zlatlan, the largest Lizardmen temple-city on the continent, almost entirely cut off from Lustria’s guiding Slann. Rarely troubled by raiders, its priesthood has developed readings of the Old Ones’ prophecies unlike any other city’s, which makes the place as valuable to scholars as to conquerors.',
  'fw-jungles-of-the-gods': 'Dense Southlands jungle whose name promises more than any expedition has confirmed, its ruins and canopy keeping most secrets exactly where they were left. What little has been recovered has been enough to keep the rumour, and the expeditions, alive.',
  'fw-dawns-landing': 'The tip of the Southlands continent, guarded by the High Elven Fortress of Dawn and its single colossal waystone. It watches the sealanes around the cape the way its sister fortress, the Citadel of Dusk, watches the tip of Lustria far to the west, and losing either weakens the other.',
  'fw-eastern-colonies': 'A scattering of Old World settlements clinging to the Southlands’ farther shore, isolated enough from the rest of the continent’s affairs that most maps barely trouble to update them. Isolation has kept them alive rather than noticed, which suits the colonists who prefer it that way.',
  // Lustria
  'fw-isthmus-coast': 'Long, jungled straits and sheltered coves where the Isthmus reaches out to sea, the preferred landing point for every Old World expedition despite the Lizardmen and the disease that greet them. Skeggi and its rotting sister ports still draw adventurers hoping the mineral wealth of Lustria will make the risk worthwhile.',
  'fw-isthmus-of-lustria': 'A narrow neck of scrubland and jungle separating the eastern and western seas by little more than a hundred miles, and the border between Naggaroth and the Lizardmen’s continent. It is the first line the Lizardmen have to hold against the warmblooded world, and everyone who has ever tried to invade Lustria has had to cross it first.',
  'fw-jungles-of-pahualaxa': 'Jungle dominated by Pahuax, the City of Ash, at the southern end of the Isthmus. Whoever wants a foothold this far north in Lustria has to reckon with a Lizardmen city that has never needed reminding of its purpose.',
  'fw-scorpion-coast': 'A stretch of Lustrian coastline claimed as a base camp by an Empire expedition, useful chiefly for the ambitions it lets outsiders stage from Lustrian soil. Every colonising power that has tried to get a permanent grip on the continent has needed a coast like this one to start from.',
  'fw-aymara-swamps': 'Swampland near Pahuax where a Cathayan sea captain once ran aground and lived only because the Lizardmen read a tattoo on his back as a mark of Sotek. The Dragon Emperor’s later expeditions came looking for the same magical currents that spared him, convinced the Geomantic Web still has something left to give.',
  'fw-creeping-jungle': 'Northern Lustria’s abandoned reaches, home to the empty temple-city of Spektazuma and the Amazon holy site at the Temple of Kara. An Empire expedition has claimed the latter as a forward base, which makes the jungle worth watching even though the Lizardmen who once held it are long gone.',
  'fw-jungles-of-green-mist': 'Jungle largely abandoned by the Lizardmen, its temple-cities in ruin since Mazdamundi reshaped Lustria’s geography to oppose the Dark Elves. The Wellsprings of Eternity are said to lie within, an untethered fountain of high magic that ancient stelae still strain to contain.',
  'fw-piranha-swamps': 'Miles of stinking mire beneath central Lustria’s canopy, its dark waters home to more than one species of flesh-eating fish and a great deal else besides. Tlaxtlan, the City of the Moon, stands as the largest settlement nearby, and nobody crosses these swamps twice by choice.',
  'fw-tarantula-coast': 'Northeastern Lustrian coastline close to Tlax, the City of Ghosts, separated from the Vampire Coast by a jungle-choked peninsula. It is a border in practice as much as geography, between the Lizardmen’s order and Luthor Harkon’s drowned dominion next door.',
  'fw-gwangee-valley': 'A central Lustrian valley holding the Mal’liente Swamps and Hualotl, the City of Masks, its golden-masked streets watched over by a sparse Lizardmen population. Nearby Chaqua once held the golden plaque describing the prophecy of Sotek, and relics like that never stop drawing attention.',
  'fw-turtle-isles': 'A tropical archipelago off central Lustria’s coast, colonised once by elven wizards fleeing civil war and later fortified by the Phoenix King as a resupply port for his western fleets. Its rare wildlife draws High Elf scholars as readily as it draws Dark Elf beastmasters after the same exotic prizes.',
  'fw-sacred-pools': 'A magical forest at the heart of Lustria, home to the Wardens of the Living Pools and whatever older purpose the pools themselves still serve. Little about it has been explained to outsiders, which has never stopped them from asking.',
  'fw-river-qurveza': 'A river wider and longer than any in the Old World, running from the Spine of Sotek to the Great Ocean near the Volcanic Islands. It forms the natural boundary between the Vampire Coast and the Lizardmen’s jungles, which makes it a border worth controlling rather than merely crossing.',
  'fw-jungle-of-webs': 'Low-lying swamp and treacherous reef along Lustria’s central eastern seaboard, rich in little but insects and disease. Luthor Harkon carved his small, dismal empire out of exactly this ground, and the Vampire Coast has answered to him ever since.',
  'fw-mosquito-swamps': 'Lizardmen-guarded swampland that has served as a strategic chokepoint for every faction that has ever needed to move through central Lustria. Nothing about it is pleasant to hold, only necessary.',
  'fw-volcanic-islands': 'A chain of jungle islands off Lustria’s east coast, currently the seat of a High Elf order of loremasters that came here for reasons it keeps to itself. Whatever knowledge is being guarded on islands this remote is worth more than the geography suggests.',
  'fw-headhunters-jungle': 'Cleared jungle near the Mangrove Coast, scored with huge, precise lines that Elven explorers have never managed to explain and Lustria’s vegetation has never reclaimed. Nothing about the lines is magical, which somehow makes the mystery worse rather than better.',
  'fw-spine-of-sotek': 'A mountain range running along Lustria’s west coast, dividing the arid southwest from the wetter jungles further north. Its name alone is enough to draw the Cult of Sotek, and where that cult goes, so does everyone hunting relics of the god it serves.',
  'fw-copper-desert': 'A hot, arid band beyond the Spine of Sotek, shielded from the coast’s humidity and the Lizardmen’s usual attention by the mountains behind it. The Golden Colossus stands somewhere within it, a Nehekharan construct that Tomb King expeditions keep sending Liche Priests to study, hoping to learn how to wake it.',
  'fw-lost-valley': 'Southern Lustria backed against the Spine of Sotek, abandoned by the major Lizardmen powers and left to whatever moved in after a demonic incursion ruined its capital. Clan Pestilens now infests the wreckage, doing to the old temple what nothing native to Lustria would have dared.',
  'fw-worm-coast': 'A stretch of Lustrian shoreline as unglamorous as its name, valued less for what it produces than for the anchorage and shelter it offers ships working the wider coast. Nobody names a coastline like this hoping to attract settlers, only sailors who need somewhere to put in.',
  'fw-culchan-plains': 'A savannah south of Oyxl where the jungle finally gives way to grassland stretching to Lustria’s southern tip, named for the giant carnivorous birds that stalk it. Skinks reportedly ride the Culchan as mounts, which tells most travellers everything they need to know about walking these plains unarmed.',
  'fw-night-forest-road': 'A jungle route cut through Lustria’s interior, one of the few links between the coast and the deeper temple-cities for travellers willing to move after dark rather than not move at all. What exactly earned it the name has never discouraged anyone determined enough to use it.',
  'fw-the-capes': 'The southerly tip of Lustria, held by High Elves since the height of their empire and shielded by the Citadel of Dusk, a fortress invisible to any eye that has not been told where to look. Its warships patrol the strait to keep every other seafaring race from reaching the Far Sea, and that alone makes the Capes worth more than the ground itself.',
  // Southern Wastes
  'fw-daemons-landing': 'The Southern Chaos Wastes’ coast, corrupted by the same collapsed Polar Gate that flooded the whole continent with Chaos, its capital of Daemon’s Gate serving as the region’s provincial seat. Isolated from the Northern Wastes by ocean rather than open ground, it has become a threat unto itself rather than an extension of the one further north.',
  'fw-ice-fire-plains': 'A province split by a great chasm in the Southern Chaos Wastes, where fire and ice sit close enough together that neither ever fully wins. The contradiction alone draws the sort of attention the Ruinous Powers favour, which is reason enough for their servants to keep contesting it.',
  'fw-abyssal-glacier': 'A frozen expanse in the Southern Chaos Wastes, its native beasts hunted for furs by whatever passes for local inhabitants this far from anything else. Little else recommends it, which has never stopped the hunters from staying.',
  'fw-daemonium-coast': 'The coastal fringe of the Daemonium, a stretch of the Southern Wastes given over entirely to Slaaneshi corruption. What washes ashore here rarely resembles what it once was, and fewer still who land expecting to leave again manage it.',
  'fw-daemonium-hills': 'Hill country southeast of the Southlands, its Crucible of Delights worked for marble and its Castle of Splendour for iron, both in the service of a Daemon Prince who treats the province as his private menagerie. Okkam’s Forever Maze anchors the region for the forces of Chaos, and finding a way through it is the least of what any invader would need to survive.',
  // Grand Cathay
  'fw-stonesky-foothills': 'A hardbitten stretch of the Eastern Steppes northwest of the Great Bastion, once Hobgoblin territory and now under Zhatan the Black’s iron grip. Granite Spikes marks where the Stonehorns of the Mountains of Mourn go to turn fully to stone in death, and Zhatan means to use this foothold to see what the Great Bastion makes of the worst the Chaos Dwarf forges can build.',
  'fw-eastern-steppes': 'Endless grassland and cold forest beyond the Great Bastion, merging without any clear line into the Realms of Chaos themselves. Kurgan and Khazag warbands wage running battles across it with each other and anyone else unlucky enough to cross their path, marking the ground with black monoliths to their fallen champions.',
  'fw-red-wastes': 'Chaos Wastes territory directly north of the Great Bastion, scarred by the countless sieges Kurgan warbands and other Chaos scourges have thrown against it over the centuries. Cathay holds a foothold here, but surrounded by enemies suited to poisoned ground it can never call the position secure.',
  'fw-central-great-bastion': 'A stretch of the colossal wall that has held the Hobgoblin Khanate and the forces of Chaos out of Cathay for longer than most empires survive. Its garrisons and animated defences are said to answer to some deeper, magical connection with the Dragon Emperor himself, and no section of the Bastion is considered spared just because the fighting is heaviest elsewhere.',
  'fw-eastern-great-bastion': 'The eastern reach of the Great Bastion, where the wall’s protection begins to thin as it nears the coast and the Chaos Wastes press closer regardless. Whatever gap opens here opens onto everything the Bastion was built to keep out, which makes even its weaker stretches worth holding.',
  'fw-gunpowder-road': 'The road running along the Bastion’s south-western reach, ruled from Nan-Gau, the City of Smoke, where forges and workshops turn out a ceaseless supply of arms for the wider empire. Every army Grand Cathay fields owes something to what moves down this road, which makes its capital as much a target as any border fort.',
  'fw-lands-of-stone-and-steel': 'A hard, industrious stretch of Grand Cathay given over to quarrying and metalwork that feeds the empire’s wider war effort. It produces more than it advertises, which suits the province and frustrates anyone trying to gauge Cathay’s real strength from the outside.',
  'fw-imperial-road': 'The route to Wei-Jin, crown jewel of Grand Cathay, where the floating Celestial City hosts the Dragon Emperor’s court and the Wu-Xing Compass that lets the Cathayan Dragons steer the Winds of Magic. Nothing in the empire matters more than what this road leads to, which makes it the most heavily watched stretch of ground in all of Cathay.',
  'fw-plains-of-xen': 'A flat, dry expanse east of the Imperial Road, exposed to the Chaos Wastes where the Great Bastion does not reach but shielded to the north by a river said to hold the spirit of a dead Dragon princess. Dark Elf pirate fleets have marked Cathay as an easy target since learning of it across the Boiling Sea, and this coast is where that ambition would have to land first.',
  'fw-forests-of-the-moon': 'Cool, tiered forest climbing the eastern Celestial Mountains, home to the Grand Observatory at Xing Po and said to be under the Moon Empress’s personal protection. Isolated Greenskin tribes and, more quietly, Clan Eshin are both rumoured to use its peace as cover, which makes the forest calmer to look at than it actually is.',
  'fw-warpstone-desert': 'A desolate waste at Grand Cathay’s western fringe, saturated with warpstone that forces caravans to travel behind mirrored shields or risk mutation. Cathayan alchemists brave it anyway for the rare ores it yields, which tells most sensible people everything they need to know about what those ores are worth.',
  'fw-jade-river-delta': 'Where the Jade River meets the Far Sea, its richest port at Fu-Chow home to the Great Dragon Fleet and a foreign population large enough to make the city one of the most cosmopolitan in the mortal world. Whoever controls this delta controls Cathay’s door to the wider ocean, and every trading nation on that ocean knows it.',
  'fw-celestial-riverlands': 'The heartland of Grand Cathay, a fertile province of rivers ruled by the Jade Dragon Yuan Bo from the trading hub of Shang Wu. It is peaceful compared to the empire’s frontiers, which only makes the danger lurking in its forests more unwelcome when it surfaces.',
  'fw-nongchang-basin': 'A fertile, low-lying stretch of the Celestial Riverlands known as Cathay’s rice bowl, its boats riding low in the water as they haul grain along the canals to every corner of the empire. Millions of mouths in Cathay’s great cities depend on what leaves this basin, which makes its harvest worth more than its modest defences suggest.',
  'fw-jungles-of-chian': 'Southern jungle where Athel Loren’s most ancient spirits journeyed long ago to find new Worldroots, making an unlikely home among Cathay’s bamboo groves. Human demand for that bamboo, especially during the building of the Great Canal, has provoked rampaging Treemen more than once, and the jungle has not forgotten it.',
  // Ogre Kingdoms
  'fw-ivory-road': 'The stretch of the Mountains of Mourn where Greasus Goldtooth first offered Cathayan caravans protection for a price, lending the road its name and his claim to being the richest Ogre alive. Every caravan crossing from Shang-Yang still pays that same toll, which has never once made Greasus consider lowering it.',
  'fw-wyrm-pass': 'A southern route through the Mountains of Mourn toward Ind, avoided by most caravans for running close to the cursed former Ogre homeland and the Great Maw itself. The Lazarghs who claim it have been twisted by the corruption seeping off the wastes, and their bells toll constantly along the pass as tribute to a god that gives nothing back.',
  'fw-gnoblar-country': 'Lush, scarcely populated hills east of the Dark Lands and south of the Mountains of Mourn, once home to Gnoblar tribes worn down by arrowhawks, Chaos Dwarf slavers and the games nearby Pigbarter plays with their lives. What is left of the Gnoblars mostly avoids the hills that used to be theirs, and everyone else has noticed the land is nearly empty for the taking.',
  'fw-haunted-forest': 'A forest at the edge of the Ogre Kingdoms, unremarkable in size but heavy enough with old magic that even the Wood Elves have taken renewed interest in it. Whatever haunts the name has never scared off everyone, which is exactly why the ritual worth attempting there keeps drawing them back.',
};
